    "millsfield:images_texts": [
      "Launching of the First Liquid\nFuel Rocket\nMarch 16, 1926 • Auburn, Massachusetts, U.S.A.\nROBERT H. GODDARD\nU.S.AIR MAIL O\n16\n1984\n01501\nUSA\n20c",
      "International Tributes to the History of Flight\n= Launching of the First Liquid Fuel Rocket\nThe epochal first flight of a liquid fuel rocket took\n\"launch pad.\"\nplace on a farm in Auburn, Massachusetts on March\n16, 1926, and lasted for just two and one-half seconds.\nThe full-color cachet on this Cover shows Goddard\nStill, it was the fulfillment of a boyhood dream for Dr.\nmaking a final check of the launch assembly used to\nRobert H. Goddard, a physics professor and rocket\npropel his rocket skyward. It was constructed of me-\nenthusiast. He sensed that he had launched the pro-\ndium bore water piping purchased at a local hard-\ntotype of a rocket that might someday carry man to\nware store. Long feed pipes connected the engines\nthe moon.\nto the fuel tanks.\nThe test flight on Aunt Effie's farm was witnessed\nMint Stamp: The mint stamp on this Cover was issued\nby the United States in 1964. It features Dr. Goddard\nby Goddard's wife, who photographed the event.\nThe professor huddled behind a flimsy wooden lean-\nand an Atlas Rocket leaving its launching tower at\nCape Kennedy (now Cape Canaveral), Florida.\nto and reached out to light the rocket with a blow-\ntorch. With a roar, the ten-foot rocket soared to\nan altitude of 41 feet. It then tumbled back to\nPassachusetts Co March seared inversary\nearth, landing in a cabbage patch 184 feet from its\nthe first successful flight of a liquid-fueled rocket.\n© Postal Commemorative Society\nPrinted in U.S.A\n150. 024"
    ],
